tgs:Monologue.Frankly_77_SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Philosophies on the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly argues the show usually engages the future through facts and synthesis, but people don't all relate to the future through the same philosophical frame — proposing seven real, distinct categories of how someone might view what's coming, offered directly as a tool for genuine empathy: knowing which category someone else occupies changes how the same facts will actually land with them."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/77-7-philosophies-on-the-future> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hyhz4q-5NKA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Frankly77Transcript7PhilosophiesontheFuture.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly precedes the seven categories with a real, separate observation: people don't even care about the same things when looking toward the future — some focused on wealthy nations, others on materially poor nations, others on non-human species, and others on real, distant future generations, decades or millennia out. He's real and direct that category six, nuanced pro-growth advocates, aren't naive: they've genuinely studied biophysical limits and civilizational complexity, but conclude a real, unplanned degrowth event would trigger cascading failure across six-continent supply chains, medicine, and spare parts — a real, direct 'Mad Max' scenario worse than continued growth's own risks. He's fully direct that his own real position, category seven, isn't advocacy for degrowth or collapse, but a real, considered acceptance that involuntary degrowth is coming regardless of preference — crediting financial credit expansion, already explored directly in Frankly_23's own 'Mordor Economy,' as the real, primary mechanism, with complexity, geopolitics, and ecological limits as real, contributing but secondary factors. Closes on a real, direct, practical point about the framework's own actual use: the goal in naming these seven isn't to rank or judge them, but to recognize that the same facts and the same synthesis will land completely differently depending on which philosophical starting point the other person in a conversation actually holds."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (Valuegraphics data on what people care about, planetary boundaries, degrowth definitions) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


