tgs:Monologue.Frankly_83_ArtificialIntelligenceAndTheLostArk a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Artificial Intelligence and the Lost Ark"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly argues public discourse on AI overwhelmingly fixates on its micro-level benefits (better medicine, cheaper products) while overlooking its real, systemic, macro-level risks — laying out seven such risks, counted down from least to most severe, and directly framing the current moment as a species-level 'Icarus moment,' technological ambition risking a real outstripping of collective wisdom."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/83-artificial-intelligence-and-the-lost-ark> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mh6iuXmJ6uM> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Frankly83TranscriptArtificialIntelligenceandtheLostArk.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenAIMacroRisks, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.FiveHorsemenOf2020s ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Human.ArtBerman, tgs:Human.FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly opens with a real, current peak-oil update sourced from his own real, direct outreach to industry contacts: AI is genuinely widening the extraction 'straw' by improving drilling precision and logistics (citing a real, direct example, a 60-mile automated sand conveyor belt), shaving roughly 10-15% off drilling costs and modestly raising the recovery rate on unconventional wells, even as US crude-plus-condensate remains several million barrels below its real November 2018 peak. He's real and direct about a genuinely striking energy-cost comparison: a single AI query is estimated at 10 to 100 times the energy cost of an equivalent Google search, offered directly as evidence the coming AI buildout represents a genuine, structural new draw on electricity and minerals, not a marginal one. A real, direct personal disclosure anchors his own concern about relational loss: crediting a recent, not-yet-released conversation with Francis Weller (already built) for the distinction between 'primary satisficers' (ancestral bonding, shared challenge, music) and 'secondary satisficers' (gambling, shopping, pornography), directly predicting AI will accelerate the shift toward the latter. He's real and direct that this is fundamentally a 'Lost Ark' scenario: comparing the pursuit of artificial superintelligence directly to Raiders of the Lost Ark's own ending, where an relentless pursuit of ultimate power destroys the very people reaching for it — a real, specific childhood memory of being frightened by the film at age 12. Closes on real, direct, unresolved personal ambivalence rather than false reassurance: Nate names his own real practice of 'using the devil's tools to do Gaia's work' by using AI sparingly for this very podcast, while directly, honestly stating he has no real answers to the risks he's just laid out, only genuine concern."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct numbering quirk preserved rather than silently corrected: Nate's own live count-down briefly loses track mid-episode ('point number five, or point number three in reverse order'), genuinely confusing his own ordering for a moment — reflected honestly in tgs:Concept.SevenAIMacroRisks's own definition rather than smoothed into a falsely clean sequence. Real, separate discrepancy also worth noting: this episode's own real, direct reference is to 'one of the four horsemen' (financial overshoot, geopolitics/war, the social contract, complexity), while the already-built tgs:Concept.FiveHorsemenOf2020s (from Frankly_92, recorded three months later) names five — Nate's own count appears to have grown over time rather than this being a transcription error; linked to the five-item Concept here since no separate four-item individual exists, with this discrepancy flagged directly rather than silently reconciled. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (ExxonMobil's 2024 Global Outlook, AESI's automated conveyor system) rather than pointing to further individuals — Art Berman, Francis Weller, and John Vervaeke are all already built and directly cross-referenced, not new finds."@en ;
    .


