tgs:Monologue.Frankly_92_AIInServiceOfLife a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Artificial Intelligence – In Service of Life?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens with a real, specific prompt: a new acquaintance, an AI investor, called asking him to help her assess whether a cutting-edge AI project she's considering funding is genuinely 'in service of life' — prompting him to unpack, for a real, dedicated Earth Week Frankly, what that phrase actually means, and to define ten direct criteria for what unambiguously is NOT in service of Life (capital L)."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/92-artificial-intelligence-in-service-of-life> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m1Rc_codNVA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Frankly92TranscriptAIInServiceofLife_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.FiveHorsemenOf2020s, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson, tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:Interview.TGS_103_PeterBrannen, tgs:Human.JamesHansen ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly traces the real, personal origin of his own central phrase: Nora Bateson once told him a story about her father Gregory Bateson telling young students, roughly 50 years ago, to make sure their lives were 'in service of life' — with Rex Weyler reportedly present at that same real, historical moment. He's real and direct that AI being genuinely in service of Life would require one of four categories, three of which he considers unlikely: changing the Superorganism's own growth-optimizing governance structure (unlikely), inventing new extraction-reducing technology (unlikely, since the rebound/backfire effect means any efficiency gain becomes a larger straw under unchanged cultural goals), or improving humanity's relational wisdom toward the natural world (genuinely possible, if a meaningful fraction of people used AI this way) — with a fourth, unknown category left open given AI's own emergent, unpredictable nature. A real, direct, extensive cataloging of Earth's biodiversity anchors his own definitional case for capital-L Life: from roughly 3 trillion trees and 73,000 tree species to 500 primate species and just 8 remaining great ape species (of which humans are one, the last of nine hominid species), offered directly as evidence for how much genuine complexity is actually at stake. He's real and direct that curing malaria without also protecting the rainforest habitats it inadvertently 'defends' from human encroachment would itself fail his own Life-with-capital-L standard, since it would arguably accelerate rainforest destruction — a real, deliberately uncomfortable example he includes despite anticipated pushback. Closes on real, direct personal vulnerability rather than confident resolution: naming his own reaction to laying out these ten criteria as 'a bit of relief' at having stated his real beliefs plainly, mixed with real trepidation, since the same criteria are directly threatening to many well-intentioned people already doing meaningful lowercase-l work (wind farms, recycling, the UN Sustainable Development Goals) that nonetheless falls short of the capital-L threshold he's describing."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct resolution of two standing gaps: this episode's own real, enumerated 'five horsemen' reference finally provides the formal source for tgs:Concept.FiveHorsemenOf2020s, first flagged as worth minting in TGS_10's own scopeNote; James Hansen (already built this session) is directly cited here for his own real, recent paper suggesting the coming warming maximum could reach 8-10°C. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tom Crowther (real, directly named upcoming guest on biocomplexity, not built, though his own real TGS episode would be a direct, traceable next build given this explicit forward reference)."@en ;
    .


